1 TITL H.E. Sahib ad-Dawla, Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Politician, Minister, and controversial (to the British protectorate regime in Egypt) Prime minister appointed by the Khedive Abbas Hilmi II against the wishes of Lord Cromer in January 1893. Born in Cairo to a fam
2 CONC ily of Circassian background, he was educated in the princely schools. In 1863 he was appointed as ''mu'awin'' (assistant) in the governorate and then transferred to foreign affairs. Sent to Paris by the Egyptian government for th
2 CONC e 1867 Exposition, he stayed there to study administrative law, returning to Egypt in 1874. He worked in the Ministry of Justice and in the ''Niyaba'' of the Mixed Courts. Hussein Fakhry became Minister of Justice in 1879-1881 und
2 CONC er Moustafa Riyad, in 1882-1884 under Mohamed Sherif, and in 1888-1891 under Riyad again; Minister of Interior under Moustafa Fahmy; and ー after serving 3 days (15-18 January 1893) as the Khedive's Prime Minister ー he would serve 
2 CONC as Minister of Works & Infrastructure and Minister of Education under Nubar and Fahmy. It was under his Ministry of Works & Infrastructure that the Nile Reservoir in Aswan and the weir at Asyut were built (contract signed on Feb. 
2 CONC 21st, 1898 between Hussein Fakhry, representing the Egyptian Government, and Mssrs. John Aird & Co., Contractors) and the the first tramway line inaugurated in Cairo. The Nile Reservoir was completed on July 1st, 1903. A lover of 
2 CONC science, he belonged to the Institut d'Égypte, the Société de Géographie d'Égypte, and the Committee for the Preservation of Arab Monuments. The public controversy over his khedivial appointment in opposition to Cromer gave Fakhry
2 CONC  a reputation as a nationalist striving to support the Khedive's wish to free Egypt from British domination. ''(mostly from "Historical Dictionary of Egypt", Arthur Goldschmidt Jr. and Robert Johnston, rev. ed. 2004)''
