1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://ru.rodovid.org/wk/%D0%97%D0%B0%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%81%D1%8C:165577
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_XII_of_Georgia Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT George XII (Georgian: გიორგი XII, Giorgi XII), sometimes known as George XIII (November 10, 1746 – December 28, 1800), of the House of Bagrationi, was the second and last King of the Kingdom of Kartl-Kakheti in eastern Georgia fro
2 CONC m 1798 until his death in 1800.
2 CONT
2 CONT Third son of King Heraclius II, he was raised in a country at war, facing regular attacks from the Persian and Ottoman empires in its south, and constant Lezgin raids from its northeast, and became heir to the throne after the ear
2 CONC ly death of his two older brothers. As prince, he was a diligent governor of royal domains, seeking to repopulate devastated regions in Georgian Armenia, while seating on his father's royal council, assisting him in leading Georgi
2 CONC an forces against Ottoman incursions, and representing him in diplomatic negotiations to bring peace to Western Georgia. However, he had to face the ambitions of his step-mother Queen Darejan and her sons, ambitions that would gro
2 CONC w into open tensions and a 1794 deal in which King Heraclius changed the law of succession to make George's younger brothers next in line after George.
2 CONT
2 CONT Taking over after his father's death in 1798, he sought closer relations with Russia as a guarantee to secure his succession, especially after reverting the 1794 deal and appointing his son David as crown prince. This led to a civ
2 CONC il war, with the king's younger brothers rebelling, and Russia dispatching troops in 1799 to restore peace in Kartl-Kakheti. An astute diplomat, he also sought alliances with Persia and the Ottoman Empire and was proposed a milita
2 CONC ry partnership by Napoleon, but was forced to use Russia's power to prevent further devastations of his kingdom by his southern neighbors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Weakened by illness, he was considered a failed monarch, paralyzed by paranoia, who could not put an end to the constant rebellions of his brothers. The poor economic state of the country led to a collapse in bureaucracy and a ris
2 CONC e in crime, while he failed to implement any of the major public, financial, and educational reforms his son Ioane proposed. Supporting his kingdom's integration into the Russian Empire, he trusted Russian diplomats without realiz
2 CONC ing their own role in dividing Georgian nobility. In 1800, he secured from Emperor Paul I an approval of what his "Petitionary Articles", which provided for Eastern Georgia's integration into Russia as an autonomous kingdom, altho
2 CONC ugh he died without knowing about its ratification. His death opened the doors for Russia to break its agreements and fully annex Georgia.
