1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Prince '''Nikolai Iwanowitch Troubetzkoy''' (February 12, 1807 - May 30, 1874) - publicist, Catholic and Slavophile; grandson of Prince D. Yu. Troubetzkoy. The owner of the estate Znamenskoye-Sadki.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT The youngest son of the chamberlain Prince Ivan Dmitrievich Troubetzkoy (1756-1827) from his marriage to the famous Moscow beauty Ekaterina Alexandrovna Mansurova (d. 1831). On his father's side, he was a cousin of Leo Tolstoy and
2 CONC  a fourth cousin of Pushkin. In the Troubetzkoy family, the poet often visited his sister in childhood.
2 CONT
2 CONT He grew up in a rich parental home on Pokrovka, where the entire literary world of the capital gathered, balls and charity performances were held. He received a good education at home under the guidance of M. Pogodin, who spent ev
2 CONC ery summer as a teacher at the Troubetzkoy Znamensky estate near Moscow. After the death of his father, he lived with his mother in St. Petersburg in the house of his uncle P. A. Mansurov.
2 CONT
2 CONT He began his service as a cornet of the Little Russian Cuirassier Regiment, and then transferred to the Life Guards Horse Regiment. But already in 1830 he retired. According to a contemporary, the young Troubetzkoy was “a very han
2 CONC dsome young man, of a rather mediocre mind, but a nice fellow, and besides, he owned five or six thousand peasants”[1].
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of his mother at the end of 1831, Troubetzkoy planned to move with his unmarried sister Alexandra to Berlin to live with his elder sister Mansurova. But their move abroad did not take place.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1837 they lived in Moscow, where in September Sister Alexandra married Prince N.I. Meshchersky, and in November Nikolai Iwanowitch married Countess Gudovich. In the early 1840s, he left Russia with his wife and settled in Franc
2 CONC e, where he bought the chateau (estate) Belfontaine [fr] near Fontainebleau (in Samois-sur-Seine). He was engaged in journalism, sometimes he spoke under the pseudonym M. Olgerdovich.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his articles he dealt with topical issues of the political life of Russia; on the question of the abolition of serfdom, he took a moderate-liberal position. Contemporaries were very skeptical of his work. Herzen found his pamph
2 CONC lets "stupid"[2]; others considered the prince an extremely limited person. S. T. Aksakov and N. A. Nekrasov satirically portrayed him in their works. The image of "Prince Koko", one of the leaders of the noble opposition, in Turg
2 CONC enev's "Smoke" is also associated with him. L. N. Tolstoy, who visited his uncle in France in 1857, called him “stupid and cruel” in his diary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under the influence of Pogodin, he showed interest in Slavophile ideas, but despite this, in 1843 he renounced Orthodoxy and converted to Catholicism. According to the writer E. M. Feoktistov, Troubetzkoy “blindly, unconditionally
2 CONC  believed in everything that Catholic priests, monks and sisters of mercy told him.” With his way of life and chaotic concepts, he made a strange impression on others[3].
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in May 1874 at his home in Belfontaine and was buried in Samua[4], in the cemetery at the chapel built by him. After his death, Turgenev cynically remarked: “Finally, the poor Prince Troubetzkoy went to make sure that what
2 CONC  his Jesuits told him was true.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife (since November 12, 1837) [5] - Countess Anna Andreevna Gudovich (1818-1882), daughter of Count, Major General Andrei Iwanowitch Gudovich (1782-1867) from marriage to the Polish beauty Ekaterina Nikolaevna Pravdich-Zalesskaya
2 CONC  (1781-1847 ). Together with her husband she lived in France, where she had a bizarre lifestyle. Married had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrei (11/01/1838 [6] - ?), baptized on November 12, 1838 in the Church of John the Theologian on Bronnaya with the reception of Prince S. M. Golitsyn and grandmother Countess E. N. Gudovich.
2 CONT Ekaterina (1840-1875), since 1858 married to the diplomat Prince Nikolai Alekseevich Orlov (1827-1885), their sons Alexei (1867-1916; general) and Vladimir (1868-1927). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT   Lopukhina M. A. Letter from Hugel A. M., October 29/30, 1837 Moscow // Russian Archive: History of the Fatherland in evidence and documents of the 18th-20th centuries: Almanac. - M .: Studio TRITE: Ros. Archive, 2001. - [T. XI].
2 CONC  - S. 200-204.
2 CONT   Herzen's letter to Turgenev dated January 05, 1861 // Complete collection. op. and letters of Herzen / ed. Lemke. - Petrograd, 1919-1925. - T. XI. - p. 6.
2 CONT   E. M. Feoktistov. Behind the scenes of politics and literature. 1848-1896. - M., 1991. - S. 67-68.
2 CONT   Anderson V. M. Troubetzkoy, Prince Nikolai [Iwanowitch] // Russian necropolis in foreign lands. Issue 1: Paris and its environs / Publisher led. book. Nikolay Mikhailovich. - Petrograd: Printing house of M. M. Stasyulevich, 1915
2 CONC . - S. 88. - XXIII, 101, [1] p.
2 CONT   Registers of births of the Church of St. Nicholas the Wonderworker in Khlynov // State Budgetary Institution of the Central State Administration of Moscow. F. 203. - Op. 745. - D. 322. - L. 669. Archival copy dated January 22, 2
2 CONC 022 on the Wayback MachineL. 670. Archived 22 January 2022 at the Wayback Machine
2 CONT   GBU CGA Moscow. F. 203. - Op. 745. - D. 328. - L. 537. Metric books of the Church of John the Theologian on Bronnaya. Retrieved 19 January 2022. Archived from the original on 19 January 2022. 
2 CONT
